# Monitoring Standards

## What to measure
- Golden signals per critical route: **latency, traffic, errors, saturation**.
- Track dependency health: PostgreSQL, Redis, queue lag, external APIs, disk, CPU, memory.
- Business counters that matter: cost confirmations, allocations finalized, AP/AR recognition, settlement success/fail, close completions, integration failures.

## SLOs & alerts
- Define SLOs (e.g. p99 API < X ms, error rate < Y%). Alert on **burn / user impact**, not every blip.
- Every alert needs a **runbook** (what it means, where to look, mitigate, escalate).
- Page only for actionable severity. Route noisy signals to dashboards/tickets.
- Wire Sev-1 to automated mitigation where safe; see `incident-automation.mdc` (3am loop + launch tabletop).

## Dashboards
- One service overview + one business overview. Link panels to logs/traces.
- Include deploy markers on charts to correlate regressions.

## Instrumentation
- Prefer OpenTelemetry metrics/traces in ASP.NET Core. Consistent label names (route, status, tenant).
- Do not high-cardinality label unbounded IDs (userId as metric label) — use logs/traces instead.

## CMS financial controls
- Monitor financial command conflicts and integration failure repeat rates continuously.
- Page a human instantly (Sev-1) on user-facing SLO burn, money-path failures, auth down, or data-loss suspicion.
- Track exception backlogs, reconciliation issues, and close blockers as business monitoring views.
- Keep business control metrics strictly separated from infrastructure alerts.
